        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "45",
        "original": "01 \/ PROTOTYPE",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "46",
        "original": "Test the product shape",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "47",
        "original": "We use AI to build an early working prototype and expose unclear requirements before they become expensive implementation decisions.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "48",
        "original": "02 \/ MODULARISE",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "49",
        "original": "Split the system into modules",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "50",
        "original": "The application is divided into bounded parts with explicit interfaces, acceptance criteria and dependencies.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "51",
        "original": "03 \/ BUILD",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "52",
        "original": "Orchestrate focused agents",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "53",
        "original": "Claude, Antigravity, Codex and Copilot are used for constrained development tasks. Work is delivered in small, reviewable iterations toward the MVP.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "54",
        "original": "04 \/ VERIFY",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "55",
        "original": "Cross-check important decisions",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "56",
        "original": "Critical architecture, security and data decisions are challenged across multiple tools, tested against the code and approved by a developer.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "57",
        "original": "Why custom",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "58",
        "original": "Own the solution, not just a subscription.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "59",
        "original": "A custom application can follow an existing workflow, connect the systems already in use and avoid paying for unrelated features. It is not always the better option; the decision depends on scope, maintenance and long-term cost.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "60",
        "original": "Example 01 · Model infrastructure",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "61",
        "original": "Pay for inference only when it is used.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "62",
        "original": "An application can call an on-demand model when a request arrives instead of paying for dedicated model capacity that remains reserved during periods with little or no traffic.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "63",
        "original": "No continuously running GPU or model server for an early product",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "64",
        "original": "Usage-based cost while traffic is low or irregular",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "65",
        "original": "Move to dedicated capacity when sustained volume makes it more economical",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "66",
        "original": "Dedicated inference can provide more predictable latency, capacity and isolation. The cost comparison depends on traffic volume and the selected model.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "67",
        "original": "Example 02 · Application knowledge",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "68",
        "original": "Use RAG when the facts change.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "69",
        "original": "Retrieval-augmented generation finds relevant content at request time and supplies it to the model. Documents can be updated without retraining the model, while the application controls access and can show the sources used for an answer.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "70",
        "original": "Update the knowledge base independently from the model",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "71",
        "original": "Filter retrieval by user, organisation or document permissions",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "72",
        "original": "Use fine-tuning for behaviour or format—not as the default way to store changing facts",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "73",
        "original": "RAG still requires good document processing, retrieval tests and rules for answers that are not supported by a source.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "74",
        "original": "Technology decisions, explained",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "75",
        "original": "What we use, where it fits and where it doesn’t.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "76",
        "original": "01 · Application runtime",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "77",
        "original": "PHP and Node.js",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "78",
        "original": "PHP fits",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "79",
        "original": "Content, commerce and transactions",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "80",
        "original": "PHP is a practical choice for WordPress, Magento, customer portals and business applications built around forms, records and relational data.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "81",
        "original": "WordPress and Magento development",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "82",
        "original": "CakePHP as our preferred rapid application development framework",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "83",
        "original": "Laravel when its ecosystem or an existing team makes it the practical choice",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "84",
        "original": "Node.js fits",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "85",
        "original": "Real-time and event-driven applications",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "86",
        "original": "Node.js is useful when the application handles live updates, queues, streaming APIs or benefits from sharing JavaScript types and packages between frontend and backend.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "87",
        "original": "Non-blocking I\/O for concurrent connections",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "88",
        "original": "Strong JavaScript and TypeScript ecosystem",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "89",
        "original": "Natural fit for API and integration services",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "90",
        "original": "They are not mutually exclusive:",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "91",
        "original": "a project can use PHP for its core business application and Node.js for a clearly separated real-time or background-processing service.",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "92",
        "original": "How we work with PHP &amp; Node.js →",
        "translated": "",
        "status": "0"
    },
    {
        "id": "93",
